    "ls|LS16_S11_U_343": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 343\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2017\nSETTING UP OF MEDIPARK\n343. SHRI YOGI ADITYA NATH:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to set up any special fund for setting up Medical Park\n(Medipark);\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the steps taken by the Government to set up such Medipark?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): No.\n(b): Does not arise in view of reply to part (a) above.\n(c): The proposal to set up Medipark at Chengalpattu (Tamilnadu) under the aegis of HL L\nLifecare Ltd, a Central Public Sector Enterprises (CPSE), under the Administrative control of\nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare, has been approved by Cabinet in its meeting held on\n5th September, 2016 HLL Lifecare Ltd has initiated necessary action accordingly.",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_344": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 344 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.03.2017 \n \nEvaluation of Educational Institutions \n \n†*344. SHRI RAVINDRA KUMAR PANDEY: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government has set up or is likely to set up mechanism for \nevaluation of the educational institutions in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; \n(b) whether the Government has made arrange ments to rank various \nuniversities/ educational institutions in the country and if so, the details \nthereof; and \n(c) the details of the top -ranked Universities/educational institutions among the \nvarious educational institutions in the country, State/UT-wise? \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SH. PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n(a) to (c):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n \n \n***** \n�\n�\n�\n�",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_345": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 345\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2017\nPROGRAMMES ON ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH ISSUES\n345. SHRI PREM DAS RAI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government is running any programmes on environmental health\nissues;\n(b) if so, the details thereof; and\n(c) the details of activities conducted, funds allocated and spent under such\nschemes/programmes, State/UT-wise during the last three years?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SMT. ANUPRIYA PATEL)\n(a) to (c): The Ministry of Health & Family Welfare has constituted an Environment and\nClimate Change Cell under the Dte. General of Health Services.  An Expert Committee under the\nChairmanship of Dr.V.M.Katoch, Ex-DG and Secretary, Department of Health Research has also\nbeen constituted for a Plan of Action for Climate Change and Human Health.  A Centre for\nEnvironment and Climate has also been established in the National Centre for Disease Control\n(NCDC).  The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare is not running any National Programme\non Environment Health.\nThe Ministry of Environment Forest & Climate Change has undertaken certain\nprogrammes/research proposals on environmental health issues.  Details of the proposals, funds\nsanctioned and released are as under:\nS. No. Name of Research proposal Fund Sanctioned\n(in Rs.)\n1st installment\nreleased (in\nRs.)\n1. Bioavailability of Dioxin like Polychlorinated biphenyls,\nDioxins and Furans released due to combustion of electronic\nwaste in Chennai: Implications for Risk Assessment\n43,71,720 24,78,600\n2. Development of GIS based decision support system for Health\nImpact Assessment due to Anthropogenic Pollution in River\nYamuna\n35,28,996 25,47,348\n3. Estimating mercury levels and exposure for pregnant women\nand new Born Babies in selected coastal and interior South\nIndian cities: first step towards building a database on risk from\nmercury pollution in India\n15,47,000 5,26,660\nTotal 94,47,716 55,52,608",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_346": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 346 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27TH MARCH, 2017 \n \nFunds to Universities \n \n †*346. SHRI VINOD LAKHAMASHI CHAVDA: \n \n Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a) the funds allocated to various universities including Kachchh University in Gujarat \nby the Government during the last three years and the current year, year -wise and \nUniversity-wise;  \n(b) the number of posts of lecturers, senior lecturers, readers and professors \nsanctioned by University Grants Commission in various universities of Gujarat; and \n (c) whether the Government has allocated funds for organising special training \nclasses and skill development programmes in the State and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n(a) to (c): A statement is laid on the Table of the House . \n*** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (A) TO (C) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. † 346 FOR 27.03.2017 ASKED BY  SHRI VINOD \nLAKHAMASHI CHAVDA, HON’BLE MEMBER OF PARLIAMENT REGARDING \nFUNDS TO UNIVERSITIES. \n \n(a):  The University Grants Commission (UGC) provides financial assistance to \nuniversities which are eligible to receive central assistance under section 12 B of the \nUGC Act, 1956.  The UGC has informed that  Kachchh University is not recognised \nunder this provision of UGC Act and therefore does not receive assistance from the \nUGC. However under the Centrally Sponsored Scheme of Rashtriya Uchchatar \nShiksha Abhiyan (RUSA), Infrastructural support for Universities is provided under \nwhich an amount of Rs. 20 cr ores has been approved for Krantiguru Shyamji Krishna \nVerma Kachchh University, Kachch in the Financial Year 2014 -15. D etails of all \nUniversities in Gujarat supported under the component of Infrastructure Grants to \nUniversities in the scheme of RUSA are at Annexure I. \n \n Further, the UGC also releases grant s to the Central Universitie s, State \nUniversities and Deemed to be U niversities. The detail of grants released to  various \nuniversities in the State of Gujarat as informed by UGC is at Annexure II. \n \n(b): The UGC has informed that it does not create and maintain data on teaching and \nnon-teaching positions in State Universiti es. These positions are sanctioned and \nmaintained by the concerned State Government.  \n \n However, in the Central University of Gujarat , UGC has sanctioned 147 teaching \nposts out of which 21 posts are of Professors, 42 posts are of Associate Professors \nand 84 posts are of Assistant Professors.  Also, for Gujarat Vidyapeeth, Ahmedabad \n(Deemed to be University), the UGC has sanctioned a total of 179 teaching posts out \nof which 19 posts are of Professors, 36 posts are of Associate Professor s and 124 \nposts are of Assistant Professors. \n \n(c): The UGC provides funds to the Central Universities and Deemed to be \nUniversities for overall development of the University including organising \nworkshop/seminar /conference etc.  UGC also provides financial assistance for skill \nbased Vocational Education & Training.  The details of financial assistance under \nvarious components of  Skill development in the state of Gujarat  as informed by UGC  \nis at Annexure-III. \n*** \n\nAnnexure-I \nAnnexure-I referred  to in part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starr ed Question No. 346  for \n2703.2017 asked by Shri Vinod Lakhamashi Chavda, Hon’ble Member of Parliament \nregarding Funds to Universities. \nThe details of all Universities in Gujarat supported under the component of Infrastructure Grants to \nUniversities under the RUSA Scheme are as under: \n (Rupees in  crore) \nUniversity Approved Amount \n(including State and Centre \nshare) \nCentral Share Released \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n \n2014-15 2015-16 2016-17 \n(till date) \nM.S. University 20 -- -- 1.625 5.1875 -- \nKSKV Kachchh University \n 20 -- -- 1.625 -- 7.375 \nSardar Patel \nUniversity \n \n \n20 -- -- 1.625 -- 7.375 \nVeer Narmad South \nGujarat University \n \n \n20 -- -- 1.625 -- 7.375 \nSaurashtra University \n \n \n20 -- -- 1.625 5.1875 -- \nGujarat University \n \n \n-- 20 -- -- 3 -- \nHemchandracharya \nNorth Gujarat University \n \n \n-- 20 -- -- 3 -- \n \n\nAnnexure-II \nAnnexure-II referred to in part (a) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 346 for \n27.03.2017 asked by Shri. Vinod Lakhamashi Chavda, Hon’ble Member of \nParliament regarding Funds to Universities \nDetails of funds released by UGC to various universities in the State of Gujarat \nunder Plan and Non-Plan Grants are as under: \n (Rupees in lakhs) \nSl.  \nNo \nName of the \nUniversity \n2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 2016-17             \n(till date) \n1 Central University of \nGujarat \n3546.47 5076.01 1058.35 -414.66* \n2 Gujarat Vidyapeeth, \nAhmedabad (Deemed \nto be University) \n2793.52 3398 2895.18 4111.66 \n3 Maharaja Krishna \nKumar Sinhji \nBhavnagar University, \nBhavnagar \n279.46 -- -- -- \n4 Gujarat University, \nAhmedabad \n418.27 -- -- -- \n5 M.S. University of \nBaroda \n492.37 -- -- -- \n6 North Gujarat \nUniversity, Patan \n299.16 -- -- -- \n7 Sardar Patel University, \nVallabha Vidyanagar \n293.61 --        -- -- \n8 Saurashtra University, \nRajkot \n287.05 -- -- -- \n9 South Gujarat \nUniversity, Surat \n358.67 --    -- -- \n10. \n \nGujarat National Law \nUniversity, Ahmedabad \n341.55 --   -- -- \n \n*During 2016- 17, the Central University of Gujarat returned an amount of \nRs.2500.00 lakhs  to UGC as t he University could not utilise the amount under \nCapital head as the Land has not been allotted. \n \n \n \n\nAnnexure-III \n \nAnnexure-III referred to in part (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred Question No. 346 for \n27.03.2017 asked by Shri Vinod Lakhamashi Chavda, Hon’ble Member of Parliament \nregarding Funds to Universities.  \nThe details of financial assistance given by UGC under various components of skill development in \nthe State of Gujarat are as under : \nCommunity Colleges \nSl. \nNo \nName of University/College    Allocation  \n(from 2014 18) \nGrant \nreleased  \n1 Hemchandracharya  North Gujarat University, Patan - \n(Gujarat)--384265 \n121.70 Lakh 18.55 Lakh \n2 N.S. Patel Art College, Anand Gujarat 106.85 Lakh 1.20 Crore \n3 Pramukh Swami Science and H.D. Patel Arts College, \nSarva Vidyalaya Campus, District-Mehsana \n239.90 Lakh 56.88 Lakh \n \nB.Voc Degree Programme \nSl. \n No \nName of University/College Allocation  \n( from  2014-17) \nGrant \nreleased \n     \n1. Hemchandracharya  North Gujarat University, Patan \n- 384265 Gujarat \n \n1.85 Crore 65.00 Lakh \n2. N.S. Patel Art College, Anand Gujarat 1.85 Crore 1.11   Crore \n3. Pramukh Swami Science and H.D.  Patel Arts \nCollege, Sarva Vidyalaya Campus, District-Mehsana \n \n1.85 Crore 65.00 Lakh \n4. Tolani Commerce College, Adipur kachchh, Gujarat 1.85 Crore 65.00 Lakh \n5. Central University of Gujarat  90.00 Lakh --- \nDDU KAUSHAL Kendra \nSl. \n No \nName of University/College Allocation \n(from 2015-17) \n Grant \nreleased \n     \n1 C.P Patel & FH Shah Commerce College, NS Patel \nCircle, Gujrat-388001 \n \n3.45 Crore 1.90 Crore \n2 Shree Shree Manibhai Virani, Smt. Navalben Virani \nScience College, Yogidham Gurukul, Kalawad Road-\n360005 Rajkot, Gujarat \n \n3.35 Crore 1.80 Crore \n \nCareer Oriented Courses in Universities and Colleges \nSl. \nNo. \nYear Allocation Fund Released \n(Rs.) \n1 2014 – 2015 Open Budget 3,78,000/- \n2 2015-2016 Open Budget 15,30,000/- \n3 2016-2017 Open Budget --- \n\n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_347": " \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n DEPARTMENT OF SCHOOL EDUCATION & LITERACY   \n  \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 27.03.2017 \n \nSmart Schools \n†*347. SHRI DEVENDRA SINGH BHOLE: \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT  be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Union Government has received proposals from various States, including Uttar \nPradesh for setting up of smart schools and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)whether the Government has accorded administrative a nd financial approval to these \nproposals and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor and the time by which the said proposals are likely to be accorded \napproval? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n \n                                    (SHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR) \n \n \n(a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n \n \n******** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN PARTS (a) TO (c) OF THE REPLY TO LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 REGARDING “SMART SCHOOLS” FOR ANSWER ON \n27.03.2017 ASKED BY SHRI DEVENDRA SINGH BHOLE   \n \n(a) to (c) The “Information and Communication Technology in School” (ICT) component of \nRashtriya Madhyamik Shiksha Abhiyan (RMSA) provides for opportunities to secondary stage \nstudents to build their capacity  in ICT skills and make them learn through computer aided \nlearning process, giving priority for coverage of schools in E ducationally Backward Blocks and \nin a reas having concentration of S cheduled Castes, Scheduled Tribes, Minority & Weaker \nSections. The Scheme covers Government and Government aided secondary and higher  \nsecondary schools. The ICT component of RMSA also has a provision for setting up of Smart \nschools to act as “Technology Demonstrators” and to propagate ICT skills among stude nts of \nneighborhood schools.  So far 88,078 schools and 43 Smart Schools have been  approved under \nthe ICT component of RMSA.  Both the components of the Scheme provide for financial \nassistance for capital expenditure as well as recurring costs, including software such as learning \nmanagement systems & curriculum based courseware.  \n \nIn case of Uttar Pradesh, 5608 schools are approved so far. 5 Smart Schools we re also approved \nin 2011- 12, but due to non- implementation of the smart schools approved under the ICT in \nSchool Scheme by the State Government, the approval of the 05 Smart  Schools was cancelled in \n2015-16. \nEvery year, the States/ UTs prepare their Annual Work Plan & Budget proposal for approval \nunder the RMSA scheme, including for the component of ICT in Schools. T he viable proposals \nunder the “ICT in Schools” component including of Smart Schools of the States/ Union Territories \n(UTs) are considered and approved under  RMSA in the presence of State/ UT concerned.  A \nstatement indicating the State -wise details of proposals r eceived and approved along with reasons \nfor non- approval for the year 2017-18 is attached as Annexure.  \n\nAnnexure \nANNEXURE REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PART (a) TO  (c) OF LOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 347 TO BE ANSWERED ON 27.03.2017 ASKED BY \nDEVENDRA SINGH BHOLE REGARDING \"SMART SCHOOLS” \n Normal schools under ICT Smart Schools under ICT \nSl. \nNo \nName of the State \n/ UT  \nProposa\nl  \nAppr\noval \n \nRemarks  Proposal  Approv\nal \n \nRemarks  \n1 A & N Islands \n45 31 Approved for 31 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nroom  \n No proposal \n2 Andhra Pradesh \n1075 - Not approved as all eligible \nschools were already covered \nunder ICT component of \nRMSA \nProposal for \nsetting up 2542 \nSmart schools \nreceived. \n- Not approved as all \neligible schools were \nalready covered under \nICT component of  \nRMSA. \n3. Arunachal Pradesh  \n21 - Not approved as previously \napproved  schools have not yet \nbeen implemented by the state \n No proposal \n4 Assam \n234 100 Approved  for 100 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nroom \nNo proposal \n5 Bihar \n488  380 Approved for 380 Schools \nhaving enrolment more than \n300 \nProposal for \nsetting up Virtual \nClassroom in 38 \nschools per \ndistrict received. \n- It was suggested that \nthe State Government \nmay meet the \nexpenditure out of \nstate funds. \n6 Chandigarh \n3 2 Approved for 2 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nroom \n No proposal \n7 Chhattisgarh \n372 -  Not approved as all eligible \nschools were already covered \nunder ICT component of \nRMSA \n No proposal \n8 Dadra & Nagar \nHaveli \nNo proposal  No proposal \n9 Daman & Diu - - - - \n10 Delhi \n1174 -  Not approved as all eligible \nschools were already covered \nunder ICT component of \nRMSA \nProposal for \ninstalling Smart \nBoards in 1034 \nSchools @Rs. \n1.25 lakh per \nschool received. \n517 \nschools \n517 schools @Rs. \n70000 per school on \npilot basis was \napproved. \n11 Goa \n56 - Not approved as all eligible \nschools were already covered \nunder ICT component of \nRMSA \n No proposal \n12 Gujarat \n217 - Not approved,  as  schools \napproved in year 2016- 2017 \nhave not been  implemented \nso far. \n No proposal \n13 Haryana \n2704 - Not approve d as all eligible \nschools were already covered \nunder ICT component of \nRMSA \n No proposal \n14 Himachal Pradesh \n2007 20 Approved for 20 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nRoom and enrolment \nProposal for \nDigital Smart \nClasses for 131 \nschools. \n It was suggest ed to \nthe State Government \nthat the project may \nbe implemented with \nstate funds. \n\n  Normal schools under ICT Smart Schools under ICT \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the State \n/ UT  \nPropo\nsal  \nAppro\nval \nRemarks  Proposal  Approval \n \nRemarks  \n15 Jammu & Kashmir \n1822 350 Approved  for 350 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nroom \n No proposal \n16 Jharkhand \n1983 449 Approved for 449 schools \nhaving electricity, Computer \nroom \n No proposal \n1