    "ls|LS16_S11_U_407": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF COAL \n \nLOK  SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 407 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017 \n \nRevision of Royalty Rates on Coal \n \n *407.      SHRIMATI RITA TARAI:  \n    SHRI NAGENDRA KUMAR PRADHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of COAL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Study Group constituted by the Government to consider the revision of \nroyalty rates on coal has submitted its report to the Government and if so, the details \nthereof \n \n(b) whether the Government proposes to enhance the rate of royalty on coal; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the time by which the decision is likely to be taken in this \nregard; and \n \n(d) whether the royalty proposed to be revised is commensurate with cess so as to benefit the \ncoal bearing States which bear the brunt of environmental degrad ation on account of coal \nmining and if so, the details thereof? \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE (IC) IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL, POWER, NEW & \nRENEWABLE ENERGY AND MINES \n \n (SHRI PIYUSH GOYAL) \n \n \n \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n  \n\nStatement referred to in reply to LS Starred Question no. 407 for 30.03.2017 \n \n(a) to (d) :-  \n \nThe Study Group, constituted to consider the revision of rate of royalty has submitted \nits report and the same is under consideration of the Government. \n \nFor the benefit of the  people of the districts where mining takes place, Government has \namended the Mines and Minerals (Development & Regulations) Act, 1957 in January, 2015 \nand introduced Section 9 B which provides for establishment of District Mineral Foundation \n(DMF). Government has prescribed an amount equivalent to 30% of royalty  for old mines \nand 10% of royalty for new mines to be paid by the holder of a mining lease to DMF. \n \nGovernment has also framed a scheme namely Pradhan Mantri Khanij Kshetra Kalyan \nYojana (PMKKKY) for the socio -economic development of the areas & districts where \nmining takes place utilizing this fund. \n \n \n \n***** \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_408": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n  \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 408 \nANSWERED ON 30TH MARCH, 2017 \n \nNHS IN ANDHRA PRADESH \n \n*408.  SHRI Y. V. SUBBA REDDY: \n  \nWill the Minister of ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \nसडक़ परिवहन औि िाजमार्ग मंत्री \nbe pleased to state:  \n \n(a) whether the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) has targeted to construct 400 \nkms  NHs in Andhra Pradesh during 2016-17 and if so, the details thereof;  \n \n(b) whether an amount of Rs. 800 crore  has been allocated for this purpose but so far the \nexpenditure  incurred is only to the extent of Rs. 150 crore; and  \n \n(c)  if so, the details thereof and the time by which said target is likely to be achieved? \n \n \n \nANSWER \n \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE \nMINISTRY OF ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS \n \n(SHRI PON. RADHAKRISHNAN) \n \n(a) to (c) A statement is laid on the Table of the House.  \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 408 ANSWERED ON 30 TH MARCH, 2017 ASKED BY SHRI Y. \nV. SUBBA REDDY REGARDING ‘NHS IN ANDHRA PRADESH’ \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- \n(a) to (c) National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) has targeted to construct 27.27 km \nin Andhra Pradesh during 2016 -17.The actual length constructed so far during 2016-17 is 27.55 \nkm and the expenditure incurred is Rs. 848.78 Crore.  \n In addition, the Ministry has also constructed 330 Km length of National Highway under \nNHDP Phase IV and NH(O) scheme , for which expenditure incurred  is Rs. 1852.51 Crore  in \n2016-17. \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_409": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 409\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017\nANOMALIES UNDER PMGSY\n*409. SHRI KAMAL NATH:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has found several anomalies ranging from poor quality to\ndelay in the road construction work being executed by its own PSU i.e. National\nProject Construction Company (NPCC) under the Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak\nYojana (PMGSY) in some States;\n(b) if so, the details thereof, State/UTwise; and\n(c) whether the Government also proposes to take action against the defaulting\ncontractors to whom work was assigned by NPCC and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nThe Statement referred in reply to parts (a) to (c) of Lok Sabha Starred Question No.\n409 due for answer on 30.03.2017\n(a) to (c): ‘Rural Roads’ is a State subject and Pradhan Mantri Gram Sadak Yojana\n(PMGSY) is a one -time special intervention of Central Government to provide rural\nconnectivity, by way of a single all -weather road, to the eligible unconnected habitations in\nthe core network.  As per PMGSY Guidelines, procurement (tendering & award of works)\nand execution of PMGSY works is responsibility of the State Government.  M/s National\nProjects Construction Corporation Limited (NPCCL) has been engaged to augment the\nlimited execution capacity of Bihar, Jharkhand, Orissa and West Bengal for execution of\nPMGSY works in these States. The State wise detail of works sanctioned & completed by\nM/s NPCCL is given below.\nSl.\nNO.\nSTATE WORKS\nAWARDED\n(ROADS AND\nBRIDGES)\nWORKS\nCOMPLET\nED\nWORKS\nTERMINA\nTED\n(1) (2) (3) (4) (5)\n1. Bihar 652 616 -\n2. Jharkhand 937 447 -\n3. West Bengal 209 83 -\n4. Orissa 19 14 5\nThere are specific complaints about the poor performance of NPCCL in terms of\ntimely completion of awarded works as per PMGSY guidelines in Jharkhand. The matter was\ncomprehensively reviewed by the Ministry on 21 st February, 2017 in presence of\nrepresentatives from M/s NPCCL, Government of West Bengal, Jharkhand, Bihar and\nOdisha.  M/s NPCCL was directed that the defaulting contr actors’ recoveries, be effected,\nthey may be blacklisted and legal action be initiated against them.  For accelerated execution\nof PMGSY works, the Ministry of Rural Development in consultation with the aforesaid\nState Governments, and M/s NPCCL has formul ated an Action Plan to substantially achieve\nthe target of the Scheme by March, 2019.\nAs per the information furnished by the State Government of Bihar, the defaulting\ncontractors have been blacklisted and Performance Securities along with other securiti es\navailable with them has been forfeited as per the programme guidelines. State Government of\nJharkhand has informed that, in respected of 23 roads in Gumla and West Singhbhum\ndistricts, police cases have been registered against the erring Officials and C ontractors for\nvarious discrepancies noticed during inspections. In view of complaints received from public\nand their representatives, with regard to quality of construction of roads 32 roads constructed\nby M/s NPCCL have been referred to ACB (Anti Corruption Bureau) of the state.   Moreover,\nM/s NPCCL has also debarred 21 contractors and another 20 has been blacklisted for various\nirregularities.",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_410": "������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�\n������ �\n�\n�������������������������������������������� �\n������������������������ �\n�\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������� ������������������\n���� ��� ������� ��� ���� ���� �������� ��� ���� ���������� ������ ������������ ��� ���� ������\n�������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������� �������� ���� ��� ��������� ��� ���� ��� ������� �������� ������ ��������������\n����������� ��� ���� �������� �������� ��������� ��� ���� ����� �������� ��������� ��������� �����\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n���� �������� ����������� ���� ��������� ��������� ��������� ���� ��� ��� �������� ������\n�����������������������������������������������������������������������������\n������\n�",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_411": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF FINANCE \nDEPARTMENT OF ECONOMIC AFFAIRS \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 411 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON FEBRUARY 3, 2017 / MAGHA 14, 1938 (SAKA) \n“DEMAND OF NEW CURRENCY” \n \n411.    SHRI MUTHAMSETTI SRINIVASA RAO (AVANTHI): \nSHRI HARISH MEENA: \nSHRI SUSHIL KUMAR SINGH: \nDR. BHOLA SINGH: \nSHRI M.K. RAGHAVAN: \nSHRI PRATHAP SIMHA: \n \nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has taken note of the increasing demand of currency post \ndemonetization and if so, the details thereof and the number and value of new currencies \nof Rs. 2000, Rs. 500, Rs. 100,  Rs. 50, Rs. 20 and Rs. 10 printed and circulated before \ndemonetization, bank-wise; \n(b) whether the Government proposes to print equal amount of new currency of Rs. 2000 \nand 500 in lieu of the withdrawn currencies and if so, the details thereof and if not, the \nreasons therefor; \n(c) the steps taken by the Government to increase the circulation of the new currencies \nalong with low denomination currencies; and \n(d) whether Government is aware that large number of counterfeit notes of  Rs.2000 is \nbeing circulated and if so, the details thereof along with the action taken thereon? \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n      ANSWER \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n(a, b& c) :   Consequent to the announcement of withdrawal of Legal Tender status of \nbanknotes of ₹ 500 and ₹ 1000 denominations from the midnight of November 8, 2016, \nthe Reserve Bank of India made arrangements for supply  of adequate quantity of \nbanknotes in various denominations to the public through the banks. \nOver the period from November 10, 2016 upto December 19, 2016, banks have reported \nthat banknotes worth ₹ 5,92,613 crore have been issued to public either over the counter \nor through ATMs. \nIn this period, the Reserve Bank has issued to the banks and their branches, for \ndistribution to the public, a total of 22.6 billion pieces of notes of various denominations \nof which 20.4 billion pieces belonged to small denominat ions of ₹ 10, 20, 50 and 100s \nand 2.2 billion belonged to higher denominations of ₹ 2000 and ₹ 500. \nAs regards printing of currency, RBI periodically estimates and reviews the demand for \ncurrency and accordingly in consultation with Government of India  indents for supply is \nplaced with the Presses. The same is a continuous process. \n \n(d) Neither such data is available with the Government nor RBI has come across any \nconfirmed reports of detection of counterfeit notes of the denomination of Rs.2000 in the \nbanking channel. \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_412": "(a) whether there has been a fall in Gross Domestic Product (GDP) during the quarter of October -December, 2016  \n     and is likely to further dip during the following quarter of January-March, 2017 due to recessionary trends  \n     triggered by demonetization drive, if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) whether International Monetary Fund (IMF) has estimated a fall in India's GDP growth, if so, the details thereof;  \n(c) the negative effects of fall in growth rate on various sectors of the economy particularly Manufacturing, Service  \n     and already declining agriculture sector;  \n(d) the corrective steps taken by the Government to boost the above sectors of the economy particularly the above  \n     said sectors to achieve high growth rate; and  \n(e) whether Reserve Bank of India (RBI) proposes to reduce the repo rate by 0.25%, if so, the details thereof and  \n      the extent to which it is likely to accelerate growth and benefit the economy?  \n \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF FINANCE \n(SHRI ARJUN RAM MEGHWAL) \n(a) As per the First Advance Estimates released by the Central Statistics Office (CSO), the economy is estimated to grow \nat 7.1 per cent in 2016 -17, as compared to the growth of 7.9 per cent achieved in 2015 -16. Data on Gross Domestic \nProduct (GDP) for the quarter October-December, 2016 and January-March, 2017 is yet to be released by the CSO. \n \n(b) The International Monetary Fund, in its World Economic Outlook Update, released in January 2017, has estimated that \nIndia’s growth in 2016-17 would be 6.6 per cent, as compared to 7.6 per cent in the previous year.  \n \n(c) As per the first advance estimates released by the CSO, the growth rate in agriculture and allied sectors, \nmanufacturing sector, services sector has been estimated to be 4.1 per cent, 7.4 per cent and 8.8 per cent respectively in \n2016-17, as compared to the corresponding figures of 0.8 per cent, 10.6 per cent and 9.8 per cent as per the first revised \nestimates for 2015-16.  \n \n(d) Apart from the interest waiver announced on 31 stDecember 2016, the agricultural sector is expected to receive a boost \nfrom the measures announced in the Budget 2017 -18 including higher target for agriculture credit , increased coverage \nunder Fasal  BimaYojana scheme and augmentation of the Long Term Irrigation Fund  set up in NABARD . The rural \neconomy is also expected to be supported by the emphasis given by the Budget on employment generation. The \nmanufacturing sector is likely to benefit from measures  announced in the Budget 2017 -18 like lower income  tax for \ncompanies with annual turnover up to Rs 50 crore; allowing carry -forward of MAT credit up to a period of 15 years instead \nof 10 years at present; further measures to improve the ease of doing business; and the scheme for creating employment \nin the leather and footwear industries. Apart from the measures listed above to encourage business and enterprise, the \nservices sector will also be boosted by the major push to digital economy and  the focus on infrastructure like construction \nand affordable housing.  \n \n(e) The decision on the Repo rate is taken by the Monetary Policy Committee of the Reserve Bank of India, taking into \naccount various factors including the inflation and the state of the economy.  \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_413": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nDEPARTMENT OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 413\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017\nHOUSES BUILT UNDER PMAY-G\n*413. SHRI M. MURALI MOHAN:\nWill the Minister of RURAL DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:\n(a) the number of houses constructed under the erstwhile Indira Awaas Yojana (IAY) and\nPradhan Mantri Awaas YojanaGramin (PMAY -G) during the last three years,\nState/UT-wise including Andhra Pradesh and Telangana;\n(b) whether the Government proposes to increase its target this year and if so, the details\nthereof; and\n(c) whether any time -line has been set for the completion of the annual target and if so,\nthe details thereof?\nANSWER\nMINISTER OF RURAL DEVELOPMENT\n(SHRI NARENDRA SINGH TOMAR)\n(a) to (c): A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n*****\n\nStatement referred to in reply to parts (a), (b) and (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred\nQuestion No. 413 due for answer on 30.03.2017\n(a) : A statement indicating number of house constructed during the last three years\nunder the erstwhile Indira Awaas Yojana (IAY), State -wise/UT-wise including Andhra\nPradesh and Telangana is at Annexure-I. Pradhan Mantri Awaas Yojana-Gramin (PMAY-G)\nhas been launched on 20th November, 2016.\n(b) : The Ministry has increased the targets under PMAY -G for the current financial year\nby 33%.\n(c) : As per Framework for Implementation of PMAY -G, the construction of house\nshould be completed within 12 months from the date of its sanction.\n\nAnnexure-I\nAnnexure refers to Lok Sabha Starred Question No 413 due for Answer on 30.03.2017\nHouse Constructed under the erstwhile Indira Awaas Yojana (IAY) duirng  the last three\nyears\n(Units in Nos)\nS.No. State 2013-14^ 2014-15^ 2015-16*\n1 ANDHRA PRADESH 206075 46722 64998\n2 ARUNACHAL PRADESH 454 110 1\n3 ASSAM 75103 99704 71830\n4 BIHAR 275869 493874 278380\n5 CHATTISGARH 29895 27274 24197\n6 GOA 616 1093 27\n7 GUJARAT 37126 65355 39752\n8 HARYANA 4532 7196 13106\n9 HIMACHAL PRADESH 6565 1620 3034\n10 JAMMU AND KASHMIR 429 1736 1989\n11 JHARKHAND 46651 30681 29877\n12 KARNATAKA 92575 104098 156539\n13 KERALA 55996 46448 50943\n14 MADHYA PRADESH 47391 45465 19459\n15 MAHARASHTRA 189602 45082 122314\n16 MANIPUR 416 1248 96\n17 MEGHALAYA 6374 10076 863\n18 MIZORAM 521 276 378\n19 NAGALAND 0 1114 638\n20 ORISSA 109844 11474 276352\n21 PUNJAB 1417 2120 227\n22 RAJASTHAN 82446 92069 64891\n23 SIKKIM 798 1538 214\n24 TAMIL NADU 69955 28869 29394\n25 TELANGANA The State was a\npart of Andhra\nPradesh\n57437 84242\n26 TRIPURA 0 23056 6248\n27 UTTAR PRADESH 157012 220739 159917\n28 UTTARAKHAND 2396 4196 7749\n29 WEST BENGAL 92071 182128 359393\n30 ANDAMAN AND\nNICOBAR\n238 148 0\n31 DADRA & NAGAR\nHAVELI\n0 0 0\n32 DAMAN & DIU 0 0 0\n33 LAKSHADWEEP 0 0 0\n34 PUDUCHERRY 0 0 0\nTotal 1592367 1652946 1867048\n^ Figures reported by States/UTs on Monthly Progress Report(MPR)/MIS\n*Figures reported by States/UTs on Awaasoft",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_414": "LOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.*414 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017 \n \nSCHEME FOR INTEGRATED TEXTILE PARKS \n \n*414. SHRI K. PARASURAMAN: \n SHRI DHARMENDRA YADAV: \n \nWill the Minister of TEXTILES वस्त्र मंरी   \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the primary objectives of the Scheme  for Integrated Textile \nParks (SITPs); \n \n(b)  whether SITP has not been able to  achieve its objectives and                                  \nif so, the details thereof; \n \n(c)  whether the Government has  constituted any committee to \nfind out the  reasons behind failure of SITP and if so,  the details \nthereof; \n \n(d)  whether the said committee has  submitted its \nrecommendations and if so, the details thereof and the action taken \nby the Government in this regard; and \n \n(e)  whether the Government is  reviewing SITP after a recent \nreport commissioned by her Ministry and if so, the details thereof? \n \nउत्तर \nANSWER \nवस्त्र मंरी (                       ) \nMINISTER OF TEXTILES \n(SMT. SMRITI ZUBIN IRANI) \n \n(a) to (e): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n  \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) \nTO (e) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *414 \nDUE FOR ANSWER ON 30.03.2017 REGARDING SCHEME \nFOR INTEGRATED TEXTILE PARKS \n \n \n(a):  The primary objectives of the Scheme for Integrated Textiles \nParks is to create integrated value -chains with world class \ninfrastructure at potential growth centres to enable the textile \nsector to realize its full potential.  The scheme aims to address \ninfrastructure constraints on cluster basis and help industry to meet \ninternational environmental standards, facilitate additional \ninvestment and generate employment. The scheme is industry \ndriven and the role of Gov ernment is limited to part -funding and \nfacilitating.  \n \n(b):  The scheme has helped to leverage investment and set up \nparks with production units and common infrastructure and \nfacilities. \n \n(c):  No Madam. \n \n(d):  Does not arise. \n \n(e):  Government has been engaged in an on -going fine -tuning \nprocess of SITP to facilitate intended development of clusters and \ngreen field investments in textiles parks. State Governments are \ninvolved in selection process and for various clearances to set up \nthese parks. \n****** ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_415": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n (DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 415 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017 \nIncentives to Sportspersons \n \n*415.  SHRIMATI MAUSAM NOOR :  \nWill the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to \nstate :  \n(a) whether the Government proposes to offer gainful \nemployment to the retired sportspersons and if so, the details \nthereof;  \n(b) whether  the Government proposes to extend the pension \nscheme to the sportspersons who have represented the country \nat International events and if so, the details thereof; and  \n(c) the number of schemes  / rewards announced by the \nGovernment along with the number of their recipients during the \nlast three years and the current year, State/ UT -wise and sport-\nwise?  \n \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \nFOR YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJAY GOEL) \n \n(a) to (c) : A statement is laid on the table of the House.   \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (c) OF \nLOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. 4 15 FOR ANSWER ON \n30.03.2017 REGARDING “INCENTIVE TO SPORTSPERSONS ” BY \nSHRIMATI MAUSAM NOOR, HON’BLE MP \n \n(a) : Recruitment of meritorious sportspersons against vacancies \nreserved for sports quota is done in Central Government offices \nin terms of extant instructions of the Ministry of Personnel, \nPublic Grievances and Pension. In terms of existing instructions \nof the Government, upto 5% of direct recruit vacancies in Group \n‘C’ and erstwhile Group ‘D’ are reserved for meritorious \nsportspersons in Central Government offices. \n \n Further, Ministries / Department of Government of India can \nrecruit meritorious sportspersons in any year in relaxation of the \nrecruitment procedure, to the extent that these, including all \nother reservations under existing orders, do not exceed 50% of \nthe total number of vacancies proposed to be filled by direct \nrecruitment. Central Governme nt organizations and Public \nSector undertakings recruit meritorious sportspersons including \nmedal winners in international sports events from time to time \nfor which they advertise in Employment News and other \nnewspapers. \n \nSports persons, who have represent ed India in Olympics / \nParalympics, are also offered employment as coaches in Sports \nAuthority of India (SAI) subject to his application, available \nvacancies and other applicable terms and conditions.  \n \n\n(b) The Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports provides  pension to \nmeritorious sportspersons after they retire from active sports \ncareer as per the provisions under the scheme of Pension to \nMeritorious Sportspersons. \n \n(c) The Department of Sports gives mainly seven awards to the \nsportspersons, coaches, entities promoting sports development \nin the country and universities with the best all round \nperformance in sports during the year of award. The year -wise \nand sports -wise details of recipients of the aforementioned \nawards are given in the Annexure. State wise da ta is not \nmaintained.  \n \n*****  \n  \n\nAnnexure \n \nAnnexure referred to in reply to part (c) of the Lok Sabha Starred \nQuestion No. 415 to be answered on 30.03.2017 regarding \n‘Incentives to Sportspersons’ by SHRIMATI MAUSAM NOOR \n \n(A ) RAJIV GANDHI KHEL RATNA AWARD  \nYear 2014 2015 2016 \nNo. of Awardees 0 1 4 \n \n \n(B) ARJUNA AWARD  \nYear 2014 2015 2016 \nNo. of Awardees 15 16 15 \n \n \n(C)  DHYAN CHAND AWARD  \nYear 2014 2015 2016 \nNo. of Awardees 3 3 3 \n \n \n(D) DRONACHARYA AWARD  \nYear 2014 2015 2016 \nNo. of Awardees 5 5 6 \n \n \n(E) RASHTRIYA KHEL PROTSAHAN PURUSKAR  \nYear 2014 2015 2016 \nNo. of Awardees 4 4 7 \n \n \n(F) Maulana Abul Kalam Azad (MAKA) Trophy  \nYear 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nNo. of Awardees 3 3 1 \n \n \n(G)  Scheme of Special Awards to Winners in International sports \nevents and their Coaches \nYear 2013-14 2014-15 2015-16 \nNo. of sportspersons/ \ncoaches given cash award  \n70 289 605 \n \n******* \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_416": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \n                MINISTRY OF SHIPPING \n \nLOK SABHA  \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.416 \n             TO BE ANSWERED ON 30thMARCH, 2017 \nACQUISITION OF NEW VESSELS BY SCI \n \n \n*416. SHRI DUSHYANT SINGH: \n \nWill the Minister of SHIPPINGbe pleased to state: \nपोतप�रवहनमंत् \n(a)  the details of Shipping Corporationof India (SCI) fleet along with the \nnumberof various classes of vessels and capacitythereof; \n(b)  whether SCI proposes to acquirenew vessels on account of ageing of its \nfleet; \n(c)  if so, the details thereof along withthe number of ageing vessels \nrequiringreplacement; and  \n(d)  the present status of fleet acquisitionby SCI along with the time-frame set \nforthe purpose? \n \nANSWER \nMINISTER OF SHIPPING \n(SHRI NITIN GADKARI) \n \n(a)to(d):A Statement is laid on the table of the House. \n  \n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION No. 416 TO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017 \nRAISED BY SHRI DUSHYANT SINGH REGARDING ACQUISITION OF NEW \nVESSELS BY SCI \n  \n(a):  The fleet position of SCI as on 24.03.2017 is as follows: \nParticulars No. of \nvessels \nGross \nTonnage  \n(GT) \nDead Weight \nTonnage \n(DWT) \nTanker - Crude Oil Carriers 16 1,099,279.00 2,017,192.00 \nTanker - Product Carriers 14 532,285.00 908,058.74 \nTankers – VLCC 5 812,551.00 1,590,809.00 \nGas carriers 2 35,556.00 35,202.00 \nBulk Carriers 16 603,808.00 1,068,088.00 \nContainer vessels 5 153,247.00 202,413.00 \nOffshore Supply vessels 10 22,958.00 23,502.38 \nPassenger-cum-Cargo 1 9,700.00 5,140.00 \nTotal 69 3,269,384.00 5,850,405.12 \n \n(b):No, SCI has replaced most of her aged fleet and has a modern and fuel efficient fleet with \nan average age of 10.19 years as on 24.03.2017, as against Indian fleet average age of \n18.4 years (INSA data as of November 2016). \n(c):  Does not arise, in view of reply to part (b) above. \n(d):SCI’s Annual Plan 2016- 17 was prepared keeping in view the immediately available \nmarket opportunities and giving due regard to the cash flow position. In line with the \nAnnual Plan, SCI has already acquired one secondhand offshore vessel  (OSV) on \n18.11.2016 and has also signed MoA for a resale Offshore Vessel (OSV). This vessel will \nbe delivered to SCI by May, 2017. One second hand Suezmax tanker will be delivered to \nSCI by March/April, 2017. In addition to this, SCI is also acquiring one  second hand \nLPG tanker and one second hand Suezmax tanker during 2017-18.  \n***** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_417": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH RESEARCH\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 417\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2017\nRESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT OF LIFE SAVING DRUGS\n417. SHRI JYOTIRADITYA M. SCINDIA:\nSHRI GAURAV GOGOI:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether a large number of people die every year due to communicable and seasonal outbreak\nof diseases;\n(b) if so, the preventive measures taken by the Government to tackle such epidemics effectively;\n(c) whether the WHO has suggested/ asked member countries including India to develop a\nblueprint for research and development of life saving R&D during epidemics; and\n(d) if so, the details thereof and the necessary steps taken by the Government in this regard?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): Yes.  People die every year due to communicable and seasonal outbreak of diseases.\n(b): With the o bjective to tackle disease outbreaks due to epidemic prone diseases, the\nGovernment has launched Integrated Disease Surveillance Programme (IDSP) which is\nimplemented in all States/Union Territories.  Besides, a network of viral research and diagnostic\nlaboratories has been set up all across the country to deal with epidemic of diseases of viral\norigin.\n(c): The World Health Organisation (WHO) has published a document in May, 2016 titled -\n“AN R&D BLUEPRINT FOR ACTION TO PREVENT EPIDEMICS - FUNDING &\nCOORDINATION MODELS FOR PREPAREDNESS AND RESPONSE, and asked the\nMember countries, including India to develop a research and development blueprint to accelerate\nlifesaving Research and Development during epidemics. The above document is available at\nWHO’s website.  Accordingly, a Coalition for Epidemic Preparedness Innovations (CEPI) has\nbeen founded by the Governments of Norway and India (the Department of Biotechnology,\nMinistry of Science and Technology, the Indian Council of Medical Research and the\nDepartment of Health and Family Welfare of the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare), the\nWellcome Trust, the Bill and Melinda Gates Foundation and the World Economic Forum in\ncollaboration with industry, other governments, foundations regulatory bodies, civil society and\nthe World Health Organisation.   The CEPI will prioritise list of diseases against which vaccines\nare most feasible to overcome barriers for development of vaccines.",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_418": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 418\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2017\nTHE ALLIED AND HEALTHCARE PROFESSIONALS'\nCENTRAL COUNCIL BILL\n418. SHRI R. GOPALAKRISHNAN:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government proposes to bring out a bill titled \"the Allied and Healthcare\nProfessionals' Central Council Bill\"; and\n(b) if so, the details thereof and the objects and reasons therefor and the present status of the bill?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a): Yes. A Bill to form council for unregulated Allied Health Professionals is under\ndiscussion.\n(b): The proposed bill is to establish a Central Healthcare Council to standardize and regulate\nallied healthcare profession education. It is under discussion in the Ministry.",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_419": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 419\nTO BE ANSWERED ON 03RD FEBRUARY, 2017\nSPECIAL BURN TREATMENT UNITS\n419. SHRI C.S. PUTTA RAJU:\nSHRI S.P. MUDDAHANUME GOWDA:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether there is shortage of separate special burn units in hospitals across the country, if so,\nthe reasons therefor;\n(b) the details of the hospitals having separate burn units in the country, State/ UT-wise;\n(c) whether the Government proposes to set up a separate burn treatment unit in all hospitals\nacross the country, if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise;\n(d) the funds earmarked, allocated and spent to establish such units during the last three years\nand the current year, State/ UT-wise; and\n(e) the time by which the said units are likely to be set up in all the hospitals in the country?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HEALTH AND\nFAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI FAGGAN SINGH KULASTE)\n(a) to (e): Health is State subject and no such information is maintained centrally. However,\na pilot programme was initiated in the year 2010 by Ministry of Health & Family Welfare in the\nname of “Pilot Programme for Prevention of Burn Injuries” (PPPBI) with a total budget of Rs.\n29 crore.  The programme was initiated in the three Medical Colleges and six Districts Hospitals\nin 3 States as 100% Centrally Sponsored Scheme.\nThe Programme has been continued during 12th Five Year Plan as ‘National Progamme\nfor Prevention & Management of Burn Injuries (NPPMBI)’ with the objective to cover 67 State\nGovernment Medical Colleges and 19 District Hospital for providing burn care units.  So far,\nBurn units have been identif ied in 44 Medical Colleges and 17 District Hospitals, details of\nwhich are available at Annexure I. The details of fund released are at Annexure-II.\nEstablishment of burns unit involves addition/alteration/modification of existing\nmodification of existing structure or construction of new structure to locate the burn unit and\nprocurement of medical equipments for which financial assistance as per the norms of the\nProgramme is provided by the Central Government. Respective State Government takes further\naction for the establishment of the Burn Units.\n\nAnnexure-I\nList of the Medical Colleges approved for establishing the Burn Units/ Wards under\nNational Programme for Prevention & Management of Burn Injuries (PPPMBI) during\n12th FYP:\nRecommended Medical Colleges\nS. No. State District Name of the Medical College\n1.\nAssam\nDibrugarh Assam Medical College & Hospital\n2. Silchar Silchar Medical College & Hospital\n3. Bihar Patna Patna Medical College\n4. Bhagalpur J.L.N. Medical College & Hospital\n5. Chhattisgarh Bilaspur Chhattisgarh Institute of Medical Sciences\n6. Gujarat Rajkot PDU Medical College\n7. Jammu &\nKashmir Srinagar SMHS Hospital & Medical College\n8.\nMadhya Pradesh\nIndore MGM Medical College\n9. Bhopal Gandhi Medical College\n10. Rewa Shyam Shah Medical College, Rewa\n11. Jabalpur Netaji Subash Chandra Bose Medical College,\nJabalpur\n12. Gwalior G.R. Medical College, Gwalior\n13.\nMaharashtra\nDhule SBH Govt. Medical College & Hospital\n14. Pune B.J. Medical College & Sasoon General Hospital\n15. Nanded S.C. Govt. Medical College, Nanded\n16. Latur Govt. Medical College & Hospital, Latur\n17. Nagpur Govt. Medical College & Hospital Nagpur\n18. Manipur Imphal Regional Institute of Medical Sciences (RIMS)\n19. Orissa Cuttack S.C.B. Medical College & Hospital\n20.\nPunjab\nPatiala Medical College and Hospial, Patiala\n21. Faridkot GGS Medical College Hospital Faridkot\n22.\nTamil Nadu\nThanjavur Thanjavur Medical College Hospital\n23. Trichirappallli KAVP Govt. Medical College & Mahatma Gandhi\nMemorial Govt. Hospital\n24. Vellore Govt. Vellore Medical College Hospital Vellore\n25. Salem Govt. Mohan Kumarmangalam Medical College\nHospital Salem\n26. Coimbatore Coimbatore Medical College Hospital, Coimbatore\n27. Madurai Madurai Medical College\n28.\nUttar Pradesh\nKanpur GSVM Medical College\n29. Lucknow K.G. Medical University\n30. Gorakhpur B.R.D. Gorakhpur\n\n31. Agra S.N. Medical College, Agra\n32. Allahabad MLN Medical College Allahabd\n33. Uttrakhand Haldwani Govt. Medical College, Haldwani\n34. Dehradun Medical College Doon Hospital Dehradun\n35.\nWest Bengal\nBurdwan Burdwan Medical College & Hospital\n36. Kolkata IPGMER, SSKM Medical College & Hospital\n37. Darjeeling North Bengal Medical College & Hospital, Darjeelin\n38.\nAndhra Pradesh\nKurnool Kurnool Medical College\n39. Guntur Guntur Medical College\n40. Tirupati Shri Venkateshwar Medicla College, Tirupati\n41. Anantpur Govt. Medical College Anantpur\n42. Kerala Kozikode Govt. Medical College, Kozikode\n43. Kottayam Govt. Medical College Kottayam\n44. Goa Goa Govt. Medical College Goa\nRecommended District Hospitals\nS. No. State District Name of the District Hospital\n1. Andaman &\nNicobar Islands Port Blair GB pant Hospital\n2. Dadra & Nagar\nHaveli Silvassa Shri Vinoba Bhave Civil Hospital\n3. Daman and Diu Daman Government Hospital, Daman\n4. Kerala Kollam A.A. Rahim Memorial District Hospital\n5. Ernakulam General Hospital Ernakulam\n6. Mizoram Aizwal Civil Hospital, Aizwal\n7. Odisha Puri District Hospital, Puri\n8. Punjab Jalandhar Civil Hospital, Jalandhar\n9. Bathinda Civil Hospital, Bathinda\n10. Sikkim Gantok STNM Gantok\n11.\nArunachal Pradesh\nNaharloung Arunachal State Hospital, Naharloung\n12. Bomdia General Hospital Bomdia\n13.\nNagaland\nKohima Naga Hospital Kohima\n14. Dimapur District Hospital Dimapur\n15. Himachal Pradesh Sirmaur RH Nahan\n16. Meghalaya Shillong Civil Hospital Shillong\n17. West Bengal Purulia Deban Mahato Sadar Hospital Purulia\n\nAnnexure-II\nDetails of funds released to establish burn units during the last three years and the current\nyear, State/ UT-wise under “National Programme for Prevention and Management of Burn\nInjuries (NPPMBI)”\nFinancial Year 2013-14\nNil\nFinancial Year 2014-15\nState Amount released\n(Rs in crore)\nAssam (under PPPBI) 0.40\nWest Bengal 5.198\nGujarat 2.59875\nTamil Nadu 3.56625\nChhattisgarh 0.20\nFinancial Year 2015-16\nState Amount released\n(Rs in crore)\nBihar 3.534\nChhattisgarh 2.39875\nManipur 3.1185\nOdisha 2 .59875\nFinancial Year 2016-17\nState Amount released\n(Rs in crore)\nAssam 6.237\nGoa 2.0790\nJammu & Kashmir 3.1185\nKerala 2.494\nMadhya Pradesh 3.741\nPunjab 2.494\nTamil Nadu 3.741\nUttar Pradesh 1.247\nWest Bengal 1.247\nTotal 36,7935",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_420": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(DEPARTMENT OF SPORTS) \n \nLOK SABHA \nSTARRED QUESTION NO.420 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 30.03.2017 \n \nSports Equipments in Training Centres \n \n*420.  SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI:  \n  Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS ; be \npleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the Government has any scheme/provision for providing \nsports equipments at various sports training centres in the country \nand if so, the details thereof;  \n(b)  whether the c ondition of sports equipments available at various \nsports training centres in the country are inspected regularly;  \n(c)  if so, the details and the outcome thereof; and  \n(d)  if not, the other measures taken/being taken by the Government \nto ensure that such equipments are main tained in working condition? \nANSWER \n \n THE MINISTER OF STATE (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) FOR YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS \n(SHRI VIJAY GOEL) \n(a) to (d): A statement is laid on the Table of the House. \n \n***** \n \n \n \n\n \nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO PARTS (a) TO (d) OF LOK \nSABHA STARRED QUESTION NO.420 TO BE ASWERED ON 30.03.2017 \nREGARDING SPORTS EQUIPMENTS IN TRAINING  CENTRES ASKED BY \nSHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI. \n \n(a)  Yes, Madam.  To promote sports in the country, Sports Authority \nof India (SAI) has established 10 Regional Centres and 02 educational \ninstitutions in the country to implement the sports promotional \nschemes in 27 sports discipline through its 289 sports centres.    \n \n To impart regular sports training to the sports person selected \nunder SAI Schemes, the provision of sports specific equipment \n(Consumable and Non - Consumable) in the identified sports discipline \nis made available in each SAI Sports Centre as per the approved  \nscheme norms.   \n \n In order to rationalize the provision of sports equipment under \nSTC & SAG Scheme, the earlier provision of Annual Maintenance Grant \nwhich contained composite provision of expenditure on civil/electrical, \nhorticulture, security, housekeep ing, water & electricity including \nsports equipment (Consumable and Non -Consumable) was delinked \nand a separate provision based on number of trainees in the centre \nwas made under Annual Grant for Consumable and Non -Consumable.  \nThis provision has come into effect from 01.7.2016.      \n \n The procurement of sports equipment for SAI Training Centres is \neither done by the respective Regional Centres or through Equipment \nSupport (ES) Division at Head Office. \n \n The funds are allocated accordingly to the respective  Regional \nCentres or to the ES division to meet the requirement by following due \npurchase procedure. \n  \n Provision of financial norms under SAI Sports Promotional \nSchemes to meet the requirement of Sports Equipment is provided at \nAnnexure. \n \n  Further, As for as sports infrastructure, equipments and other \nsports facilities required for the preparation for the elite athletes for \n\nInternational events is concerned there is no shortage. Under the \nScheme of “Financial Assistance to National Sports Federation’s”  all \nrequisite facilities including state -of-the-art equipment are provided to \nthe eminent sportspersons.  \n \n \n SAI organises National Coaching Camps for the elite sports \npersons / teams preparing for International competitions.  SAI Centres \nwhere such train ing camp are organised have infrastructure, \nequipment, services of support staff and facilities at par with \ninternational standards.   Wherever various SAI STCs are being used \nfor holding national camps, at such  places international standard \ninfrastructure, services and facilities are provided.  \n \n(b)  to (d): The sports equipment to be utilized for imparting regular \nsports training to SAI scheme trainees is made available either in the \nform of Consumable sports eq uipment or Non -Consumable sports \nequipment.  The condition of the sports equipment is   inspected and \nmaintained on regular basis in all SAI sports centres.  In fact, the \nconsumable sports equipment which is worn out over period of time is  \nreplaced with fresh equipment at regular intervals.  Similarly, the Non -\nConsumable sports equipment is also inspected and maintained \nregularly for its best use. \n \n  \n***** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nANNEXURE \n \nANNEXURE REFERRED IN REPLY TO PART  (a) OF LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION \nNO.420 FOR 30.03.2017 REGARDING SOPRTS EQUIPMENTS IN  TRAINING CENTRES \nASKED BY SHRIMATI MEENAKASHI LEKHI, MP. \n \n \nSAI Sports Promotional Schemes to meet the requirement of Sports Equipment  \n \nS.No. Name of the Scheme Particulars Amount \n1 National Sports \nTalent Contest \n(NSTC) Scheme \nAnnual grant for purchase of \nsports equipments to the SAI \nadopted regular schools & Schools \nadopted for indigenous games. \n \nAkharas adopted under SAI \nAkhara Scheme are provided a set \nof Wrestling mat and Multigym. \n \nRs.20,000.00 \n \n \n \nRs.6,50,000.00 \n(appx.) \n2 Army Boys Sports \nCompany (ABSC) \nScheme \nAnnual grant to the ABSC Centres \nfor purchase of sports equipment \nper centre per annum  \n \nRs.5,00,000.00 \n3 SAI Training Centre \n(STC) Scheme  \nAnnual grant for sports equipment \n(consumable & non-consumable) \nto STC & SAG Centres based on \nthe number of trainees in the \ncentre is as under: - \n \nS.No.   Number of trainees \n \n1. Upto 50 trainees \n2. 50-100 trainees \n3. 100-150 trainees \n4. Above 150 trainees \n \n \n \n \n \n \nAmount \n \nRs.05.00 Lakhs \nRs.08.00 Lakhs \nRs.10.00 Lakhs \nRs.12.00 Lakhs \n4 Special Area Games \n(SAG) Scheme \n5 Extension Centre of \nSTC/SAG Scheme \nInfrastructure and equipment \nsupport to the Extension Centres  \n \nRs.01.00 Lakhs \n***** ",
    "ls|LS16_S11_U_421": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nDEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 31ST MARCH, 2017\nNEW INITIATIVES IN HEALTH SECTOR\n*421. SHRI P.R. SUNDARAM:\nWill the Minister of HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has devised new initiatives and policies for the overall\ndevelopment of the health sector in the country and if so, the details thereof;\n(b) the total amount spent on various schemes/programmes during the last two years and\nthe current year, State-wise; and\n(c) whether the Government has chalked out any comprehensive health plan and new\nstrategy to involve all the stakeholders including the Non -Governmental Organisations\nand Non Profit Organisations in the implementation of health programmes and schemes\nin the country, particularly in rural areas and if so, the details thereof?\nANSWER\nTHE MINISTER OF HEALTH AND FAMILY WELFARE\n(SHRI JAGAT PRAKASH NADDA)\n(a) to (c):  A statement is laid on the Table of the House\n\nSTATEMENT REFERRED TO IN REPLY TO LOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. 421* FOR 31ST MARCH, 2017\n�������������� ���������������������� �������� ���������������������� �������� ������������������ �������������� ���������������� ������������ ����������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n��������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n�������������������� �������� �������������������� �������������� ������ ���������� ���������������� �������������� ���������� ������������������ ��������������\n������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������\n����������������